  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nA weak upper level disturbance will continue to move \nacross the Great Capital Region this afternoon with clouds becoming \nmore scattered in nature towards the evening. A few lingering shower \nchances will exist into this evening, mainly in the southern \nAdirondacks. Any showers will taper off by midnight. Any gusts this \nafternoon/evening will diminish after midnight tonight as well as \nbrief high pressure build in. Much of tomorrow looks to remain dry \nwith partly to mostly cloud skies. Temperatures will warm into the \nlow to mid 50s across the Mohawk and Hudson Valleys. Highs in \nthe mid 30s to low 40s are expected across the higher terrain of\nthe southern Adirondacks and southern Greens. Clouds will build\nin from the southwest ahead of our late week system.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nThe overall synoptic pattern for later this week is \nbecoming a bit more clear with trends toward seeing at least a \nwidespread light to moderate precipitation event. An upper level \ntrough will build across the eastern half of the CONUS on Thursday \nwith multiple shortwaves rotating around the base of the trough. An \ninitial area of low pressure will approach from the Ohio Valley on \nThursday which will primarily bring a period of rain to the region. \nThermal profiles during the day Thursday look to support some snow \nshowers across the higher terrain of the southern Adirondacks and \nsouthern Greens with the initial surge of moisture. Some light \naccumulations in the highest peaks to around an inch or less appear \nlikely through the daylight hours on Thursday. By Thursday night, an \ninitial shortwave rounding the base of the trough will reach the mid-\nAtlantic coast and take on a negative tilt. This will support the \nprimary inland low to weaken and develop a coastal low (Nor'easter) \noff the coast. \n\nWhile there is still some uncertainty with the track and timing of \nthis coastal low, it does look progressive as it tracks \nnortheastward up the coast toward Atlantic Canada by Friday. \nHowever, a secondary shortwave may take on a negative tilt off the \nmid- Atlantic coast later Friday which may induce another coastal \nlow to form taking a similar path to the first. Confidence in this \nsecondary coastal low developing has come into better agreement with \nthe daytime model guidance this afternoon. As it does so, good \nmoisture advection off the Atlantic will keep atmospheric profiles \nsaturated in the lower levels, though some drier air aloft is \nbecoming apparent with less saturation in the DGZ. All in all, this \nfavors periods of precipitation across most of the region later this \nweek into this weekend. Initial thermal profiles will favor mostly \nrain initially, but as colder air gradually filters in from the \nwest, precipitation will likely mix with and change over to a wet \nsnow, especially across the higher elevations. Valley areas likely \nremain rain through Friday with a possible mix or changeover to snow \nby Friday night. However, by this weekend, precipitation intensity \nmay be fairly light that accumulations would be unlikely for valley \nareas, especially on paved surfaces. Guidance does favor some light \nsnowfall amounts across the higher elevations (especially above 1500 \nfeet). Current probabilistic guidance paints a 60 to 70 percent \nchance for at least 2 inches of snow across the higher terrain of \nthe southern Adirondacks and southern Greens. Trends will continue \nto be monitored over the next few days with additional adjustments \nto the forecast likely as details on this event become even more \nclear. This system looks to fully depart the area by Sunday with \njust a few lingering lake effect and lake enhanced/upslope snow \nshowers mainly for areas outside of the Hudson Valley.",
